  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.104,
            0.137,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "102"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "MATHEMATICS"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.147,
            0.61,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us now find the distance between any two points "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { P } ( \\boldsymbol { x } _ { 1 } , \\boldsymbol { y } _ { 1 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbb { Q } ( \\boldsymbol { x } _ { 2 } , \\boldsymbol { y } _ { 2 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Draw PR and QS perpendicular to the x-axis. A perpendicular from the point P on QS is drawn to meet it at the point T (see Fig. 7.5)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.248,
            0.598,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Then, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{OR}=x_{1}, \\mathrm{OS}=x_{2}. \\quad \\mathrm{So}, \\quad \\mathrm{RS}=x_{2}-x_{1}=\\mathrm{PT}."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.273,
            0.608,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Also, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{SQ} = y_{2}, \\quad \\mathrm{ST} = \\mathrm{PR} = y_{1}."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " So, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { Q T } = \\boldsymbol { y } _ { 2 } - \\boldsymbol { y } _ { 1 } ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.299,
            0.618,
            0.319
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, applying the Pythagoras theorem in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta   \\mathrm { P T Q } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " we get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.633,
            0.145,
            0.898,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.633,
                0.145,
                0.898,
                0.328
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.383,
            0.205,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.298,
            0.323,
            0.609,
            0.407
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} PQ ^{2} &=  PT ^{2} +  Q  T ^{2} \\\\&= (x_{_2} - x_{_1})^{2} + (y_{_2} - y_{_1})^{2} \\\\ PQ  &= \\sqrt{\\left(x_{_2} - x_{_1}\\right)^{2} + \\left(y_{_2} - \\underline{y}_{_1}\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.715,
            0.338,
            0.801,
            0.357
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 7.5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.417,
            0.9,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that since distance is always non-negative, we take only the positive square root. So, the distance between the points "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{P}(x_1, y_1)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{Q}(x_{2},\\overline{y}_{2})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.463,
            0.632,
            0.494
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{PQ} = \\sqrt{\\left( x_{2} - x_{1} \\right)^{2} + \\left( y_{2} - y_{1} \\right)^{2}}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.503,
            0.466,
            0.522
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "which is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "distance formula",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.528,
            0.216,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Remarks :"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.553,
            0.861,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. In particular, the distance of a point "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { P } ( x , { \\hat { y } } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " from the origin O(0, 0) is given by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.345,
            0.584,
            0.519,
            0.614
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_15.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{OP} = \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.62,
            0.704,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. We can also write, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{PQ} = \\sqrt{\\left(x_{1} - x_{2}\\right)^{2} + \\left(y_{1} - y_{2}\\right)^{2}}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.663,
            0.9,
            0.702
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 1 :",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Do the points "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(3,2),(-2,-3)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and (2, 3) form a triangle? If so, name the type of triangle formed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.707,
            0.899,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Solution :",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let us apply the distance formula to find the distances PQ, QR and PR, where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "P(3,2),Q(-2,-3)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and R(2, 3) are the given points. We have"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.758,
            0.755,
            0.785
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_19.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{PQ} = \\sqrt{(3 + 2)^2 + (2 + 3)^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{50} = 7.07   (\\mathrm{appmax.})"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.795,
            0.84,
            0.821
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_20.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{QR} = \\sqrt{(-2-2)^2 + (-3-3)^2} = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + (-6)^2} = \\sqrt{52} = 7.21 (\\mathrm{appox.})"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.832,
            0.773,
            0.858
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_21.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{PR} = \\sqrt{(3 - 2)^2 + (2 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{1^2 + (-1)^2} = \\sqrt{2} = 1.41 (\\mathrm{appmax.})"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.868,
            0.9,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since the sum of any two of these distances is greater than the third distance, therefore, the points P, Q and R form a triangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
